{"name":"mssql/key-value-string-ssh","target":"saved","op":"connect","changedBy":["5","6"],"notes":"Row 7a: MSSQL builds from the fields and ignores the key=value string, so nothing parses it and Saved connects directly (v1 needed the tab). Row 5 drops the tab's empty tunnel fields; row 6 adds the TLS server name. Row 6, checked live (Task 2): the TLS name is tls_server_name while the socket goes to 127.0.0.1:<tunnel port> (the MSSQL crate's `tests/tls_server_name.rs` reads it from the ClientHello's SNI). Through the SSH container to `sqlserver`, `prefer` connects; `require`, `verify-ca` and `verify-full` fail on the certificate itself (`invalid peer certificate: UnsupportedCertVersion`: the container's `CN=SSL_Self_Signed_Fallback` is an X.509 v1 certificate with no subject alternative name), exactly as they do without the tunnel, never on the name.","input":{"row":{"id":"conn-mssql-key-value-string-ssh","projectId":"default-seaquel","labelIds":[],"savePassword":true,"saveSshPassword":true,"saveSshKeyPassphrase":false,"name":"MSSQL","type":"mssql","host":"sql.internal","port":1433,"databaseName":"sales","username":"sa","sslMode":"require","connectionString":"Server=tcp:sql.internal,1433;Database=sales;User Id=sa;","sshTunnel":{"enabled":true,"host":"bastion.example.com","port":22,"username":"deploy","authMethod":"password"}},"secrets":{"db:conn-mssql-key-value-string-ssh":"pw","ssh:conn-mssql-key-value-string-ssh":"ssh-pw"},"supplied":{},"createIfMissing":false,"tunnelPort":51433},"expected":{"secretsRead":["db:conn-mssql-key-value-string-ssh","ssh:conn-mssql-key-value-string-ssh"],"tunnel":{"sshHost":"bastion.example.com","sshPort":22,"sshUsername":"deploy","authMethod":"password","password":"ssh-pw","remoteHost":"sql.internal","remotePort":1433},"config":{"driver":"mssql","host":"127.0.0.1","port":51433,"database":"sales","username":"sa","password":"pw","encrypt":true,"trust_cert":false,"tls_server_name":"sql.internal"}},"v1":{"file":"connect-config/mssql.json","output":"reconnectTab"}},
